decisions:
  - id: stack-tauri2
    decision: Build on Tauri 2 (React/TS frontend, Rust core) rather than Electron
    rationale: User wants a lightweight Mac app now and mobile eventually; Tauri 2 is small/native and can target iOS/Android from mostly the same codebase, whereas Electron is heavier and mobile would be a separate app.
  - id: alarm-scheduler-in-app
    decision: Fire alarms with an in-app JS scheduler that dedupes by calendar-minute key
    rationale: 'Simple and reliable per-minute firing that never double-triggers on repeated renders or missed ticks. Known limitation: only fires while the app window is open — tray/background firing is the planned next step.'
  - id: local-files-asset-protocol
    decision: Play local media via native file dialog + Tauri asset protocol (persisted absolute path) instead of an <input type=file> object URL
    rationale: Object URLs don't survive restarts, so an alarm set today couldn't replay the same file tomorrow. Persisting the absolute path and serving it through convertFileSrc makes file alarms durable.
  - id: audio-autoplay-unlock
    decision: Resume a shared AudioContext on the first user gesture and rely on WebKit's post-interaction media allowance
    rationale: WebViews block audio autoplay until the page has seen a user gesture; since the user always interacts while setting an alarm, priming the context lets a later alarm actually make sound.
learnings:
  - Tauri 2 asset protocol must be enabled in tauri.conf.json security.assetProtocol with a path scope before convertFileSrc URLs will load local media.
  - WebKit blocks audio autoplay until the document has had a user gesture; priming an AudioContext on first interaction is enough to let a scheduled alarm sound later.
  - Paradigm's purpose validator existence-checks a flow's steps[].component refs against local-file scope only, so valid cross-file refs surface as cosmetic warnings even though the scan-index resolves them correctly.
